---
title: 'Maps - Geographic Areas'
enterprise: true
---

The Map Shape Series visualises data representing geographic areas such as countries, using colours to denote distinct series or the magnitude of the values.

## Simple Map Shapes

{% chartExampleRunner title="Multiple Series" name="multiple-series" type="generated" /%}

To create a Map Shape Series, use the `map-shape` series type and provide data and [topology](./map-topology/). These can be provided in either the chart or series objects.

```js format="snippet"
topology: topology,
series: [
    {
        type: 'map-shape',
        data: pacific,
        idKey: 'name',
        title: 'Pacific',
    },
    // ...
],
legend: {
    enabled: true,
}
```

In this example:

- The `topology` is provided once on the chart level, and the `data` is provided in each series.
- `idKey` defines the property key in the data that will be matched against the property value in the topology.
  See [Connecting Data to Topology](./map-topology/#connecting-data-to-topology) for more details.
- `title` provides a name for the series, and is used in the [Legend](./legend/) and [Tooltips](./tooltips/).

## Gap

{% chartExampleRunner title="Gap" name="gap" type="generated" /%}

To separate neighbouring shapes, use `gap`. It is the distance in pixels between the shapes.

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            type: 'map-shape',
            data: pacific,
            idKey: 'name',
            title: 'Pacific',
            gap: 4,
        },
        // ... other series
    ],
}
```

In this configuration:

- `gap` is set to 4 on every series, so neighbouring states are 4 pixels apart, within a time zone and across time zones.
- Coastlines and other edges with no neighbouring shape stay where they are.

Each shape moves in by half the gap along every border it shares with a shape drawn by a Map Shape Series. A shape with `gap: 4` next to a shape with `gap: 0` leaves 2 pixels between them.

The following apply when using `gap`:

- A shape that is narrower than its share of the gap is not drawn, but it can still be hovered and clicked.
- Shapes are neighbours only when they share exact border coordinates, as shapes from one topology do.
- An enclave, such as Lesotho within South Africa, is separated only when the shape around it has a matching hole. An enclave drawn over a shape with no hole for it gets no gap.
- Shapes in a series that is hidden, and shapes in a Map Shape Background Series, are not neighbours. Borders next to them behave like coastlines.
- The default `stroke` is the colour of the chart background, so the visible separation between shapes is `gap` plus `strokeWidth`. Set `stroke` to another colour to see the borders and the gap separately.
- The hover glow covers part of the gap on the highlighted shape's side. With the default glow, a `gap` of 4 leaves 3 pixels of background around the highlighted shape.
- Where a shape has a narrow part with a large `gap`, the outline can overlap itself and paint a little outside the gap.

## Colour Scale

{% chartExampleRunner title="Heatmap" name="heatmap" type="generated" /%}

To colour the shapes based on the magnitude of the data, use `colorKey`.

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            type: 'map-shape',
            idKey: 'name',
            colorKey: 'gdp',
        },
    ],
}
```

In this configuration:

- `colorKey` is set to 'gdp', which supplies numerical values for the Colour Scale.

{% note %}
See the [Colour Scale](./colour-scale/) page for more details about customising colours, discrete bins, and the Gradient Legend.
{% /note %}

## Labels

{% chartExampleRunner title="Labels" name="labels" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            type: 'map-shape',
            idKey: 'name',
            labelKey: 'code',
        },
    ],
}
```

In this configuration:

- `labelKey` defines what will appear as the title for each tile.

{% note %}
See [Label options](#reference-AgMapShapeSeriesOptions-label) for options for handling long labels.
{% /note %}

## Background Shapes

The Map Shape Background Series displays all the shapes of a topology without requiring any data.

This can be useful to show disabled Map Shape Series when toggled off in the legend, or to provide context to [Map Line Series](./map-lines/) and [Map Marker Series](./map-markers/).

{% chartExampleRunner title="Backgrounds" name="backgrounds" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
topology,
series: [
    {
        type: 'map-shape-background',
    },
    // ...
]
```

{% note %}
As this is a background series rather than a data series, many normal series behaviours are disabled - including interactivity and appearing in the legend.
{% /note %}

